[2m[2mSession[0m: [1;33mdeepseek_deepseek-v4-flash-integration-bug-trial-3[0m[0m
All verified. Summary of the fix:

**Root cause** — the original flow was check-stock → **charge** → decrement. Under concurrency, all 12 orders passed `check_stock` before any stock was reserved, so the first ~9 charges succeeded but only 5 decrements could. Result: orders charged with no item delivered (`$900–1100` charged vs `$500` expected), and in any variant where the check and decrement aren't contiguous (threads, or a sleep between them), stock goes negative.

**Changes** (public interfaces untouched; methods added only):

- `checkout.py:16` — **reserve stock first**, then charge. There is no path that charges without an item secured, and no failure path after a successful charge.
- `inventory.py:19` — added `reserve()` (atomic check+decrement — contiguous, no `await` between, so stock can never go below zero) and `release()` (returns reserved stock when payment fails).
- `payments.py:29` — added `charge_once()` (idempotent per order_id under a lock, so the same order can never be charged twice, even if submitted concurrently or retried).

**Verification** — reproduced the ghost-charge bug first (every run showed the mismatch), then after the fix:

- `main.py` × 10: no ERROR lines; `Total charged == Expected charge` in every run; stock ≥ 0; 0 duplicate charges.
- Stress tests (all pass): 20 concurrent submissions of the same order_id → 1 charge, 1 delivery, 9 stock released back; multi-unit orders; 100 orders vs 3 stock; mixed duplicates + multi-unit + 30% payment failures; 50 iterations with 40% failure rate. All invariants held: stock never negative, charges == successful deliveries, no duplicate order charges.
